    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the hierarchy for coding atypical dermatological presentations. We're seeing a lot of cases with irregular, mottled pigmentation and unusual textural changes that don't quite fit the standard L-code classifications for common dermatitis. Can you clarify the protocol for deciding between a specific highly-detailed code versus a more general 'unspecified' code when the morphological features of the lesion are atypical or don't follow a standard pattern?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Generally, you should always strive for the most specific code possible as it provides the most accurate medical record. When features are truly atypical and do not map clearly to existing detailed codes, consult your institution's coding guidelines or a peer-reviewed dermatology reference. If adherence to established guidelines is impossible, using a suitably coded \"unspecified\" category is preferable to leaving the field blank, but always document why the detailed coding was not feasible.",
      "source": "target",
