    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for the clarification. To be more specific, we are seeing some lesions that present with a very odd, greenish-grey mottled hue and a somewhat scaly, uneven texture that almost looks... well, it's just very irregular. If the morphology is that bizarre and doesn't match any common dermatological pattern in the ICD-10 index, at what point does the documentation shift from a specific skin condition to a more generalized 'other specified disorder of skin'? Is there a threshold for how much 'abnormality' is required before we move away from the standard L-series codes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The shift away from standard L-series codes usually occurs when the morphology points towards a systemic issue or a condition not independently classified within dermatological diseases. Since \"greenish-grey mottling\" is highly unusual, you should document all observable features thoroughly in the clinical notes to support coding decisions. Unless you can link the color or texture to a specific known pathology, using a code under \"Other specified disorders of skin\" is often appropriate to capture the non-standard presentation.",
      "source": "target",
